<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The app's user-visible text.

  Compose Multiplatform's string resources, which is this project's `strings.xml`: the same file, the
  same `<string name=…>` shape and the same `%1$s` placeholders, read from `composeResources/values` so
  a platform that is not Android reads them too. Every screen reaches them through
  `stringResource(Res.string.…)`, which means a screen has no sentence of its own in it — no Kotlin
  object of `const val`s, and nothing user-visible coming out of `data` either.

  The modules from the earlier slices (`ui.timetable.TimetableStrings`, `EnergyCacheHint.message`, …)
  still carry their text in Kotlin; they are the older arrangement and the mechanical follow-up, and this
  file is where new text goes.

  Keys are grouped by page, and the `%1$s`-style placeholders are the only thing a screen passes:
  anything longer than a format string is built by the caller and interpolated as one argument, so a
  translation never has to reorder a sentence's punctuation.
-->
